The COVID-19 pandemic in South Africa was marked by significant social and economic disruption.
The country's response was complicated by the limited availability of reliable, localized data necessary for decision-making.
This research explored alternative sources of pandemic-related data in South Africa, evaluating both free and paid options.
A case study was conducted to assess the effectiveness of crowdsourced data, utilizing publicly accessible Google Forms to gather information about testing sites in Johannesburg.
Analysis of the resulting data highlighted the feasibility of developing accurate models for predicting COVID-19 transmission using readily available data sources.
This investigation contributed to a broader understanding of the capabilities and limitations of non-traditional data streams, prompting officials to reconsider their potential in informing public policy. For instance, the Western Cape province's Health Department showed interest in the methodology developed here.
Furthermore, this project has implications for data-driven innovation, suggesting that productive collaborations can arise from the intersection of data science and social science perspectives on data sharing. 
A key outcome of this work is the Data-backed Decisions Framework, a guiding principle for policymakers and practitioners seeking to incorporate data-driven insights into their decision-making processes. 
This research will continue to evolve, with plans to expand the investigation to other South African provinces. 
