The fundamental auditory structure, the auditory unit, is the smallest building block for spoken language. Research into the relationship between auditory units and synthesis units has primarily been conducted within languages that use an alphabet, with significant progress made through the development of large datasets. However, creating large datasets in languages without an alphabet is extremely challenging due to a lack of standardization in transcriptions. To overcome this issue, this paper proposes an innovative method that starts with raw audio and identifies the auditory units within which speech is decomposed, entirely without relying on knowledge of the target language. Transcripts of these units are then used to train a neural synthesis system based on an alphabet that does not use written characters, such as the International Phonetic Alphabet. Experiments on speech synthesis in three languages without an alphabet - Dharwi (Mypa), Latu (Myvy), and Tawala (Mypa) - demonstrate high quality speech synthesis within these languages, further validating the concept of auditory units as the primary structure for spoken language. The projects' source code and data are publicly available for inspection. 

