A common issue in machine learning is the trade-off between optimizing performance on available data and achieving generalization. We investigated how abundant features, extracted using a Siamese network, can address this challenge in supervised learning. In experiments with MNIST, we found that networks trained on abundant features showed improved test set accuracy compared to those trained on the original 2D image data, especially when the model complexity exceeded its optimal predictive capacity. These results suggest that feature abundance can help models avoid overfitting by allowing for the optimal selection of features and model parameters. The benefits of abundant features were also observed in supervised learning tasks other than classification, such as regression and ranking. The code and features are available on GitHub for reproducibility. Additional findings and potential future research directions are discussed in the supplementary material. 

