To capture dimensionality reduction and underlying patterns in Type Ia Supernovae spectral time series, a novel stochastic autoencoder design is introduced. The proposed method leverages the inherent redundancy in spectral time series, expressing each data point as a probabilistic combination of lower-dimensional latent variables. The resulting generated data exhibits lower entropy compared to randomly generated data, indicating a substantial reduction in dimensionality. Through experiments, the developed stochastic autoencoder demonstrated superior density estimation skills on synthetic datasets and generated datasets based on real Type Ia Supernovae spectral data, outperforming traditional density estimation methods. Moreover, the proposed method demonstrated improved classification accuracy in low signal-to-noise ratio scenarios when applied to binary classification tasks involving real Type Ia Supernovae spectral temporal series. A comprehensive implementation details is provided in an accompanying document. The generated code is available on GitHub at the specified link. The proposed method has potential applications in various astrophysical spectral series datasets. 

