We develop a new algorithm, PrimDual CMP, for solving Markov decision process problems with constraints. This method leverages a constrained Markov competition procedure that we introduce, which subgrad-based constraints enforcement and relies on a non-decreasing, additive, subgrad-consistent function for optimization. By applying established theory, we prove that PrimDual CMP achieves convergence under certain conditions. Furthermore, our theoretical results, coupled with its simplicity and flexibility, make PrimDual CMP a versatile technique that can be applied in various situations. We demonstrate the efficacy of PrimDual CMP by adapting it to solve a constrained Markov process problem related to capacity planning in contact centers. When compared to existing approaches on a set of realistic problem instances, PrimDual CMP exhibits superior performance, taking significantly less time to converge while yielding nearly identical solutions. We make the implementation of PrimDual CMP, along with the problem instance data, publicly available. 

